  "heat": 2,
  "text": "A pregnant runaway slave is the first person in the Bible to give God a name. Sarai cannot have children. So she gives her Egyptian handmaid Hagar to Abram, to get a child through her. Nobody asks Hagar anything. She is a solution to someone else's problem. She conceives. Tension explodes. Sarai says to Abram, \"My wrong be upon thee.\" And Abram, husband of the year, says, \"Behold, thy maid is in thy hand; do to her as it pleaseth thee.\" He hands her back. And the text says Sarai dealt hardly with her. So Hagar runs. Pregnant. Alone. No plan, because there is not one. And the angel of the Lord finds her by a spring of water. Not a temple. Not a camp. A spring, in the middle of nowhere. And he says, \"Hagar, Sarai's maid, whence camest thou? and whither wilt thou go?\" Where did you come from. Where are you going. She answers the first one. \"I flee from the face of my mistress Sarai.\" She does not answer the second. She has nowhere to go and they both know it. Then he tells her to call the boy Ishmael, because the Lord hath heard thy affliction. And then Hagar does something nobody in Scripture has done yet. She names God. \"Thou God seest me.\" A runaway. A foreigner. A slave. And she is the one who gets to say it first.",
